#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0601487-2cc9-5ae6-aba6-ab2ef75badd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307692} {:answer/id #uuid "3942dabf-b913-5840-8d0d-9b399dc4ec6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307693} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c4c1b0-f47d-5750-8b1e-2ed53ab0587b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307694} {:answer/id #uuid "005ae058-7214-5a7b-aba6-49fed4a0328d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307695} {:answer/id #uuid "62994270-e8cc-504d-b12a-6812b6b6ca22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9c4c1b0-f47d-5750-8b1e-2ed53ab0587b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307694} :answer-field/id #uuid "5704e1b3-ce2d-5cbe-80f9-0267792c253a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c407c7a-f52a-554c-8e95-8465a14c8781" :question/math-academy-id "q-120712" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/2da1a7590ff9c8d403c040680c89f9bf310030d31399be7bd37209ef745a5bbd.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(4,-4)$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-4}{4}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20819b41-67a2-5b7f-9867-e375985fe85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307699} {:answer/id #uuid "36e43af0-4e3b-57f7-b547-8abf5f44af31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307700} {:answer/id #uuid "913ea399-1fc5-5f4a-a21f-1193ef384609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307701} {:answer/id #uuid "a9730ffb-ade1-58b4-ac44-f48b23b73db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8\\sqrt{3},-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307702} {:answer/id #uuid "56f7130b-d735-5df5-bf6a-d79ea5239c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12\\sqrt{3},-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e43af0-4e3b-57f7-b547-8abf5f44af31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307700} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f291c82-fb61-573e-b69d-b8bb4bc2eed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9300a06e-224a-5760-bf8d-1bf574e7c275" :question/math-academy-id "q-120719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5301351-f3bd-52a5-947f-cca1cb8e05a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5301351-f3bd-52a5-947f-cca1cb8e05a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307706} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a844af0-2205-5ab8-9808-063dc88aee5a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9a133c1-2916-570f-b68c-988d4cc04c8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-321322" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/c922feb998c41aae1ab40e3e6f240b07756699d4756b91458ff8271cec152f7f.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the ray shown above. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nThe polar equation is $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "772c389b-afdf-5989-a41a-2cafb9b07ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{2},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307709} {:answer/id #uuid "efb51f91-43df-54fa-98a9-cd61d9edfb08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307710} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebbd268-9d09-5ab5-9e04-659d1754505e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307711} {:answer/id #uuid "6278ff11-5619-5b6e-8d7c-504638d722a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307712} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3fc8b4-1567-5d2d-854b-103177319572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ebbd268-9d09-5ab5-9e04-659d1754505e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307711} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c526ea6-640c-5bb7-9e0a-555b082c1241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcc2043b-54b0-52ae-b83e-ebaa5b4ad306" :question/math-academy-id "q-41787" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/7a3c82568cfaf845708a9dcee79a3ac2e0e9d2131776fd1c7daa1c598473f3b3.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=10$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(10,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5\\sqrt{3},-5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec8f012-4ed2-52de-b3a0-7b7f50c94af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307716} {:answer/id #uuid "84688193-ce7d-51c2-ac01-e8d152389ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307717} {:answer/id #uuid "dc805390-9abf-5f6f-8121-b7c1ae42e7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307718} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc6991b-0ac3-5fda-bc50-185160b7f02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307719} {:answer/id #uuid "107d6ff3-45d7-5b64-a584-89049c5e7a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec8f012-4ed2-52de-b3a0-7b7f50c94af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307716} :answer-field/id #uuid "80659fd2-55b4-5467-bd0a-018342344258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b7e4f86-ed4a-5213-ac2e-f26cf9fc765f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87135" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/5507850932bb4061cd19cdbffa18aacf2b235293e643170ee78c4326a96e050d.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(9,-3\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{9}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}]}
